# Escrow claim double-counted its lease

**DONE.** Closed before these files carried a status word. The `.done`
suffix is the record; this line is that same answer in the second place
the check reads, and no new judgement.

Found in the 2026-08-25 review, one day after 002 landed. Recorded
because the SUITE WAS GREEN.

## Problem

Detach deliberately does not decrement entry.leases - leaving the
count untouched is what keeps an ownerless handle alive with no
holder. Bind, adopting those leases back out of escrow, incremented
it. So one lease became two.

Detach -> claim -> release therefore left the entry at 1, forever. The
sweeper could not take it either: a handle with leases is by
definition held. Every detach/claim round trip leaked its handle for
the life of the process - the exact pattern the evaluation server
(016) is built around.

## Why the tests missed it

test_lifecycle claimed escrowed handles and called them. It never
RELEASED after claiming, so the count never had to reach zero. All
seventeen checks passed over a permanent leak.

## Fix

Bind moves the escrowed lease onto the connection instead of adding
one.

HandleTable.audit() now states the invariant the whole model rests on:
entry.leases equals what connections plus escrow hold, because every
operation is a MOVE between those three places. A randomized loop over
put/release/share/detach/claim/sweep holds it across 300 trials; the
old code fails it on the second step.

test_lifecycle gained the missing wire-level check: releasing a
claimed lease must drop the handle.

## Lesson worth keeping

The bug was a counter that only ever went up. Every check asserted
that something still WORKED; none asserted that something had stopped.
Reachability tests pass over leaks by construction - see 012 for the
property test this argues for.
